ree distinct lines, exactly two of which are sunny; denote the non‑sunny line by \\(L_{0}\\) and the sunny ones by \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny line containing three points  \n\nThe only non‑sunny lines that pass through three of the six points are  \n\n* \\(y=1\\) (contains \\(A,D,F\\));\n* \\(x=1\\) (contains \\(A,B,C\\));\n* \\(x+y=4\\) (contains \\(C,E,F\\)).\n\nIn each case the three points **not** on \\(L_{0}\\) are:\n\n* for \\(y=1\\): \\(\\{B,C,E\\}\\);\n* for \\(x=1\\): \\(\\{D,E,F\\}\\);\n* for \\(x+y=4\\): \\(\\{A,B,D\\}\\).\n\nAny two of the three remaining points are collinear on a line whose slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence every line through any pair of them is non‑sunny, so the three points cannot be covered by the two sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\).  \nThus **no** configuration exists with \\(|L_{0}\\cap\\{A,\\dots ,F\\}|=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Non‑sunny line containing exactly two points  \n\nAll unordered pairs of the six points that lie on a non‑sunny line are:\n\n| type | line | points |\n|------|------|--------|\n| horizontal | \\(y=1\\) | \\(A,D,F\\) (three points – already handled) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=2\\) | \\(B,E\\) |\n| vertical   | \\(x=1\\) | \\(A,B,C\\) (three points) |\n| vertical   | \\(x=2\\) | \\(D,E\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\(B,D\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\(C,E,F\\) (three points) |\n\nHence the only *two‑point* non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}\\in\\{y=2,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=3\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe treat each separately.\n\n* **\\(L_{0}: y=2\\)** (covers \\(B,E\\)).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{A,C,D,F\\}\\).  \n  Check all possible sunny lines through these points.  \n  The only pairs that give a sunny slope are \\((C,D)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) and \\((B,F)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)), but \\(B\\) is already used.  \n  Any line through any other pair is either horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Consequently the four points cannot be partitioned into two sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(L_{0}: x=2\\)** (covers \\(D,E\\)).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{A,B,C,F\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair among them is \\((B,F)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  After using that line, the leftover points \\(\\{A,C\\}\\) are vertical, i.e. non‑sunny.  \n  No other sunny pair exists, so covering is impossible.\n\n* **\\(L_{0}: x+y=3\\)** (covers \\(B,D\\)).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{A,C,E,F\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair is \\((A,E)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  The remaining points \\(\\{C,F\\}\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.  \n  Hence a covering with two sunny lines does not exist.\n\nThus no configuration works when \\(|L_{0}\\cap\\{A,\\dots ,F\\}|=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑sunny line containing exactly one point  \n\nThe only non‑sunny line that passes through a single point of the set is \\(x+y=2\\), which contains only \\(A\\).  \nThen the five points \\(\\{B,C,D,E,F\\}\\) must be covered by the two sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\).\n\nA sunny line can contain at most two of these five points, because a direct inspection of all triples shows that no three of \\(\\{B,C,D,E,F\\}\\) are collinear on a sunny line (any three collinear points among them have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \nConsequently two sunny lines can cover at most four points, leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nAll possible ways to choose the unique non‑sunny line lead to a contradiction.  \nTherefore **there is no set of three distinct lines that covers the six required points and contains exactly two sunny lines**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines do