nals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{\\,n+1-k}\\) (non\u2011sunny), and  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is still \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every lattice point with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n3. The construction works as long as the indices are valid, i.e. as long as \\(n+2-t\\ge 2\\) for all \\(t\\le k\\). This is equivalent to \\(t\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\). Hence the construction succeeds for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\nCombining the upper bound with the explicit construction yields the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \nSuch a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\):\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them,\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous,\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); it meets \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) in the points with \\(a+b=c\\), i.e. \\(c-1\\) points, and \\(c-1\\le n\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence if a configuration uses \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the total number of distinct points that can be covered by those lines is at most \\(mn\\).  \nSince all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nmn\\ge |\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf the configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(m=n-k\\).  \nFrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsequently no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lie on exactly one of the lines \\(D_s\\); the family \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and therefore gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nNow fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) we replace the diagonal \\(D_{\\,n+2-t}\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{t}\\,x+\\Bigl(n+2-\\frac{1}{t}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n**Claim 1.**  The line \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n\nIndeed its slope is \\(-\\frac1t\\).  For every \\(t\\ge1\\) we have \\(-\\frac1t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_t\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n**Claim 2.**  The line \\(S_t\\) contains exactly the points of the diagonal \\(D_{\\,n+2-t}\\).  \n\nThe two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,\\;n+2-2t),\\qquad \nQ_t=(t+1,\\;n+1-2t)\n\\]\n\nsatisfy \\(P_t+Q_t\\) coordinates sum to \\(n+2-t\\); therefore both lie on \\(D_{\\,n+2-t}\\).  \nA direct substitution shows that they also satisfy equation (6), so they belong to \\(S_t\\).  \n\nBecause the slope of \\(S_t\\) is \\(-\\frac1t\\), the set of integer points on \\(S_t\\) is precisely the set of integer points on the segment joining \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\); this segment is exactly the set of points of the diagonal \\(D_{\\,n+2-t}\\) (the points of that diagonal have coordinates \\((t+i,\\;n+2-t-i)\\) with \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n+1-2t\\)).  Hence \\(S_t\\) covers all points of \\(D_{\\,n+2-t}\\).\n\nConsequently, after performing the replacements for all \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_k=\n\\bigl\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{\\,n+1-k}\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) consists of\n\n* \\(n-k\\) diagonals (non\u2011sunny) and  \n* \\(k\\) lines \\(S_t\\) (sunny),\n\nhence exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nBy Claim\u202f2 every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies either on one of the retained diagonals or on the corresponding sunny line, so all points are covered.\n\nThe construction is valid as long as the indices in (7) make sense, i.e. as long as \\(n+1-k\\ge2\\).  Condition (5) guarantees this, because \\(k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) implies \\(n+1-k\\ge\\frac{n+3}{2}\\ge2\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction of Section\u202f2 shows that every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis indeed attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the set above.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}}\n\\]"
