rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n2. **Maximum coverage by non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(t\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   For any choice of \\(t\\) lines of type (1) the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that they can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   H(t)=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}(n+1-i)=\\frac{t\\,(2n+1-t)}2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Overlaps are unavoidable when the lines are of different\n   types; taking all \\(t\\) lines of the same type (e.g. the horizontal\n   lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=t\\)) gives the largest possible number of distinct\n   points, which is exactly the sum in (2). ∎  \n\n3. **How many points must be covered by sunny lines?**  \n   Put \\(k\\) = number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of\n   non‑sunny lines.  \n   By (2) the non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(H(t)\\) points, hence the\n   points that still have to be covered by the sunny lines are at least  \n\n   \\[\n   R=|\\mathcal P_n|-H(t)\n     =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-k)(2n+1-(n-k))}2\n     =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **exactly** the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\)) must be covered by the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **A geometric restriction for the triangle \\(S_k\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a line that is not of type (1).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(S_k\\) the line \\(L\\) contains at most two points\n   of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  Write the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with\n   \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   Points of \\(S_k\\) satisfy \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence\n   \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  \n   Suppose three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\;(i=1,2,3)\\) of \\(S_k\\) lie\n   on \\(L\\).  Subtracting the equations for two pairs gives\n   \\((b_i-b_j)=m(a_i-a_j)\\).  Because the three \\(a_i\\)’s are integers\n   between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\), the differences \\(a_i-a_j\\) are integers of\n   absolute value at most \\(k-1\\).  The same holds for the differences\n   \\(b_i-b_j\\).  Hence \\(|m|=\\bigl| (b_i-b_j)/(a_i-a_j)\\bigr|\\le\n   (k-1)/(1)=k-1\\).  On the other hand, from the inequalities\n   \\(b_i\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n   \\(b_i\\le n+1-a_i\\le n\\).  Consequently\n   \\(|b_i-b_j|\\le k-1\\) as well, and the only rational slopes that can\n   be realised by three integer points of the triangle are\n   \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Since \\(L\\) is not of those three types,\n   a contradiction follows.  Hence at most two points of \\(S_k\\) lie on a\n   sunny line. ∎  \n\n   Lemma 1 is the crucial replacement for the false statement used in\n   the earlier draft.\n\n5. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most two points of the\n   triangle \\(S_k\\).  Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most\n   \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_k\\).  From (3) we need  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le 2k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k(k+1)\\le 4k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can have more than three sunny lines**.\n\n6. **Excluding the case \\(k=2\\).**  \n   For \\(k=2\\) we have \\(t=n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.  Taking the optimal\n   horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) leaves exactly the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n-1),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(1,n). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of the points in (5) is of type (1)\n   (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)), therefore a sunny line can\n   contain **at most one** of them.  Consequently two sunny lines cannot\n   cover the three points, and a configuration with \\(k=2\\) does not\n   exist.\n\n7. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  All of them are\n   non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\); they miss only\n   the point \\((1,n)\\).  Add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=2x+n-2\\qquad(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1)\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((1,n)\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\).  The six points\n   that remain uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &P_1=(1,n),\\;P_2=(2,n-1),\\;P_3=(3,n-2),\\\\\n   &P_4=(2,n-2),\\;P_5=(1,n-2),\\;P_6=(1,n-1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: P_1\\text{ and }P_4\\quad\\text{slope }-2,\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: P_2\\text{ and }P_5\\quad\\text{slope } 1,\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: P_3\\text{ and }P_6\\quad\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes \\(-2,1,-\\tfrac12\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the\n   three lines are sunny.  Together with the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines\n   all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and exactly three lines are\n   sunny.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (4) shows that \\(k\\le3\\); Lemma 1 together with the\n   counting (3) excludes \\(k=2\\); explicit constructions give\n   configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\).  Therefore for every integer\n   \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is 