an cover at most 3 points. So each must cover exactly 3 points. Let's try to find lines covering 3 points each.\n\nConsider slope -1/2? Already we saw at most 2 points. Slope 2? At most 2 points. Slope -2? Already used one line; maybe another line with slope -2 can cover 3 points? Let's check c=9: y = -2x + 9 => (1,7) not allowed; (2,5) not allowed; (3,3) already covered; (4,1) allowed? For x=4, y = -8+9 = 1 => (4,1) allowed. So line contains (4,1) only? Actually also (2,5) not allowed, (3,3) already covered, (5,-1) not allowed. So only (4,1). So not helpful.\n\nConsider slope 1 again: y = x + c. For c = -1: (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) not allowed. So (2,1),(3,2) => 2 points. c = -2: (3,1),(4,2),(5,3) but (5,3) not allowed. So (3,1) already covered, (4,2) left. So line contains (4,2) only new. c = -3: (4,1),(5,2) but (5,2) not allowed. So (4,1) left. c = -4: (5,1) left. So slope 1 lines can cover at most 2 new points each.\n\nConsider slope -1? Forbidden.\n\nConsider slope 0? Forbidden.\n\nConsider vertical lines x = c: forbidden.\n\nThus we need to find other slopes that can cover 3 points among the remaining points. Let's try slope 3: y = 3x + c. For x=1, y=3 + c ≤5 => c ≤2. For c=2: (1,5) already covered. For c=1: (1,4) left, (2,7) not allowed. So only (1,4). So not helpful.\n\nSlope -3: y = -3x + c. For x=1, y=c-3 ≤5 => c ≤8. For c=8: (1,5) covered; (2,2) covered; (3,-1) not allowed. So only (2,2) covered. Not helpful.\n\nSlope 1/3: y = x/3 + c. For integer points, x must be multiple of 3. Let's try c=0: (3,1) covered; (6,2) not allowed. So only (3,1). Not helpful.\n\nSlope -1/3: y = -x/3 + c. For x=3, y = -1 + c. Could get points (3,2) if c=3? Actually -3/3 + c = -1 + c = 2 => c=3. So line y = -x/3 + 3 => points: x=3 => y=2 (3,2); x=6 => y=1 (6,1) not allowed; x=0 => y=3 (0,3) not allowed (a>0). So only (3,2). Not helpful.\n\nSlope 2/3: y = (2/3)x + c. For integer points, x must be multiple of 3. For x=3, y=2 + c. Choose c=0 => (3,2) left. For x=6, not allowed.